D gene.\" The label depends on syntactic role, not on the surface string.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Disease Name As Premodifier Of A Gene/Protein Head**\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A disease name (full or abbreviated, e.g., \"breast cancer,\" \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome,\" \"DTD\") that serves as the leftmost constituent of a gene or protein name (\"…susceptibility gene,\" \"…syndrome protein,\" \"DTD gene\"). The model absorbs the disease string into a non-schema gene/protein identifier and outputs O, even though the disease substring functions as a Modifier.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positive Modifiers attach the disease term to a clinical, population, or phenotype head (\"ovarian cancer cases,\" \"ovarian cancer risk,\" \"G6PD deficient phenotype\"). Those heads are not gene or protein nouns, so the disease term is recognized as a disease modifier. Standalone or parenthetical disease mentions are labeled SpecificDisease.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** If a disease name or its abbreviation is a premodifier within a noun phrase whose head (possibly after intervening modifiers such as \"susceptibility,\" and possibly including a trailing number such as \"gene 1\") is a gene, protein, or locus noun, label only the disease substring as **Modifier**. Do not label the gene/protein head, and do not default to O. Apply the rule in this order:\n    1. Head is gene/protein/locus → disease substring = Modifier.\n    2. Head is a clinical, population, risk, family, or phenotype noun → disease substring = Modifier.\n    3. Disease term stands alone or heads its own noun phrase → SpecificDisease.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, 